{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf455TKWE1UZ5TnmbAe7i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1625,"cache_read_input_tokens":15781,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1625},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0abf1529-dfad-44b4-923a-1389e8368909","uuid":"ce1a69f8-ee2f-4b8e-880d-b6a2a307e996","timestamp":"2026-09-14T21:50:39.981Z","request_id":"req_011Cf455SaM7GuRSf5KtWSL4"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf455TKWE1UZ5TnmbAe7i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C4tBmvPEq9TmWKW2sRNDHf","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat iw54pyk7/.agents/skills/task-spec/references/authoring-workflow.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1625,"cache_read_input_tokens":15781,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1625},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0abf1529-dfad-44b4-923a-1389e8368909","uuid":"332a377c-797e-40e6-a9ac-2e5fa4bbf941","timestamp":"2026-09-14T21:50:42.500Z","request_id":"req_011Cf455SaM7GuRSf5KtWSL4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01C4tBmvPEq9TmWKW2sRNDHf":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat iw54pyk7/.agents/skills/task-spec/references/authoring-workflow.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C4tBmvPEq9TmWKW2sRNDHf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Authoring workflow — the task-spec doctrine\n2\t\n3\t> This is the **authoring doctrine** for the task-spec engine. The installer ships\n4\t> it as `references/authoring-workflow.md` inside every installed skill. It is\n5\t> invoked via the canonical root `SKILL.md`, the compatibility skill in\n6\t> [`harness/claude-code/`](../harness/claude-code/SKILL.md), or by\n7\t> calling the `taskspec` CLI directly. Script references below map to\n8\t> `bin/taskspec` subcommands (`new`, `batch`, `validate`, `gate`, `run`,\n9\t> `accept`, `transition`, `ready`, `rebuild-state`, `archive`, `backup`,\n10\t> `lint`, `metrics`, `conformance`); script files live under `src/<verb>/`.\n11\t\n12\t\n13\t> **Identity:** The open, atomic unit-of-work format for autonomous agentic systems\n14\t> **Domain:** Task PRD generation, eval-driven development, backlog management\n15\t> **Default Threshold:** severity-scaled (0.80–0.99) — see Threshold Mapping below\n16\t\n17\tThis skill is part of the **CAW Triad** for task generation:\n18\t- **C** (this Skill): the workflow + bundled scripts + templates\n19\t- **A** (Agent): `task-architect` — applies Agreement Matrix, ensures quality\n20\t- **W** (MCPs): Context7, Exa, Ref — fresh validation at authoring time\n21\t\n22\t---\n23\t\n24\t## When to invoke this skill\n25\t\n26\tTrigger conditions (Claude auto-invokes when context matches):\n27\t- User says \"create a task,\" \"scaffold a task,\" \"decompose this into work\"\n28\t- User has fuzzy intent (\"verify the deploy pipeline works\")\n29\t- User wants to convert a meeting note into actionable backlog\n30\t- User mentions Task-Spec, EDD, eval-driven, or \"make this executable\"\n31\t- User asks for a task that any conformant engine (e.g. Claude/Codex/Cursor) can pick up\n32\t\n33\tPause authoring if:\n34\t- The outcome is still too fuzzy to declare behaviors and discriminating evals\n35\t- An L leaf has multiple independent done-conditions (decompose it)\n36\t- Subjective output has no reviewable machine or human oracle yet\n37\t- User just wants a one-off prompt, not a reusable spec\n38\t\n39\t---\n40\t\n41\t## Workflow\n42\t\n43\t```text\n44\t┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n45\t│  TASK-SPEC WORKFLOW                                               │\n46\t├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n47\t│  Phase 1: UNDERSTAND    parse intent, classify effort             │\n48\t│  Phase 2: RESEARCH      optional AuthoringEvidence v1             │\n49\t│  Phase 3: SCAN          read host repo for touches_paths          │\n50\t│  Phase 4: ARCHITECT     spawn task-architect agent for judgment   │\n51\t│  Phase 5: COMPOSE       TaskPlan preview, then declared specs     │\n52\t│  Phase 6: VALIDATE      structural linter — validate-task-spec.sh │\n53\t│  Phase 7: GATE          PRE-gate — safe-to-delegate.sh --stamp    │\n54\t│  Phase 8: DISPATCH      hand off to executor per execution_backend│\n55\t│  Phase 9: ACCEPT        POST-gate — accept-task.sh --stamp        │\n56\t└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n57\t```\n58\t\n59\t> **The loop is closed (v3).** Phase 7 certifies the SPEC is delegate-safe (evals\n60\t> well-formed; assertion failure expected). Phase 9 certifies the WORK is real\n61\t> (evals now PASS from a clean checkout + change set within blast radius + sign-off\n62\t> HMAC intact). A task is provably DONE only when `accept-task.sh` writes\n63\t> `accepted: true`. Humans sit at Phase 1 (intent) and review the Phase 9 verdict —\n64\t> not in the middle of the execution loop. See\n65\t> [concepts/conformance-levels.md](concepts/conformance-levels.md).\n66\t\n67\t> **Alternative: Batch Sprint Compose** — when you need N specs at once in a\n68\t> known domain, skip Phase 2 (MCP research) and Phase 4 (task-architect spawn).\n69\t> See [Batch Sprint Compose](#batch-sprint-compose-fast-batch) below.\n70\t\n71\t### Phase 1 — Understand\n72\t\n73\tParse the user's intent. Critical questions answered before scaffolding:\n74\t\n75\t1. **What's the verbal description?** (1 paragraph from user)\n76\t2. **Effort class?** XS/S/M/L are leaves; L needs one coherent done-condition and a configured long-horizon backend. XL/XXL are child-composing nodes, not executor work. See `concepts/effort-gate.md`.\n77\t3. **Agent hint?** `any` (vendor-portable) OR specific (`python-developer`, etc.)\n78\t4. **Source provenance?** Meeting note, audit, ticket — must have one\n79\t5. **Touches what paths?** Best guess; refined in Phase 3\n80\t\n81\tIf unclear, ASK ONCE for clarification. Don't proceed with vague intent.\n82\t\n83\t### Phase 2 — Research (optional evidence)\n84\t\n85\tWhen current external evidence is necessary, explicitly select a provider and\n86\tnormalize its output to `AuthoringEvidence/v1`:\n87\t\n88\t```text\n89\tFirecrawl / Tavily / Exa / another explicit provider → bounded, cited evidence\n90\t```\n91\t\n92\tThe research output feeds the `anti-patterns` and `do-not-touch` zones —\n93\tboth should be informed by real-world failure modes from MCP findings.\n94\t\n95\t### Phase 3 — Scan\n96\t\n97\tRead the host repo to refine:\n98\t- Which files actually exist on `touches_paths`?\n99\t- Any cross-cutting concerns (touches the same files as in-flight tasks)?\n100\t- Existing conventions (CLAUDE.md, .editorconfig, project style)?\n101\t\n102\tThis uses Glob + Grep + Read in the host repo — no writes.\n103\t\n104\t### Phase 4 — Architect (spawn the agent)\n105\t\n106\tSpawn the **`task-architect`** agent with:\n107\t- The intent\n108\t- Phase 2 research findings\n109\t- Phase 3 repo scan findings\n110\t\n111\tThe agent:\n112\t- Applies Agreement Matrix (KB + MCP)\n113\t- Decides isolation class (worktree vs branch)\n114\t- Drafts the 3 evals as runnable bash\n115\t- Drafts anti-patterns + do-not-touch from research\n116\t- Returns a structured build plan with confidence score\n117\t\n118\tIf confidence < 0.95, ASK the user before composing.\n119\t\n120\t### Phase 5 — Compose\n121\t\n122\tRun `taskspec new` (src/author/generate-task-spec.sh) to create the T-*.md file from the template,\n123\tthen fill in:\n124\t\n125\t| Zone | What goes in |\n126\t|------|--------------|\n127\t| Frontmatter | id, title, status=ready, format_version=3, profile, effort, budget=15, agent, touches_paths, source_note |\n128\t| Zone 1 | Why / Goal / Context (lean) |\n129\t| Zone 2 | 3+ evals + validation_card YAML + exit_check bash |\n130\t| Zone 3 | Rollback Plan (or `(none)`) |\n131\t| Zone 4 | Observability Hooks (or `(none)`) |\n132\t| Zone 5 | Anti-patterns + do-not-touch list |\n133\t| Zone 6 | Open questions (or `(none)`) |\n134\t\n135\t**Two shape rules govern every atom:**\n136\t\n137\t- **Cut tracer bullets, not layers.** For feature work, prefer a **vertical\n138\t  slice** — a narrow but COMPLETE path through every layer the feature\n139\t  touches (schema → transform → serving → test), demoable or verifiable on\n140\t  its own — over a horizontal slice of one layer. A horizontal atom (\"all the\n141\t  models\", \"all the endpoints\") passes its own eval while proving nothing\n142\t  end-to-end; a tracer bullet's eval exercises the seam the system actually\n143\t  ships through. The eval defines *done*; the vertical cut defines *worth\n144\t  doing alone*.\n145\t- **Size to one fresh context window.** An atom must fit — spec, cited ADRs,\n146\t  touched files, and the working diff — inside a single fresh executor\n147\t  session. If holding the task means the executor must page out what it read,\n148\t  the atom is too big: split it and wire `depends_on`. This is the practical\n149\t  ceiling within each runnable XS/S/M/L leaf, not a new class.\n150\t\n151\t**Layered policy (legacy tolerance):** `format_version` is `3` for new specs (the template's default; see `src/templates/task-spec.md.tpl`). Tasks created before 2026-05-27 (or explicitly marked `format_version: 0`, `1`, or `2`) are treated as legacy. The validator accepts legacy v0/v1/v2 tasks with warnings rather than hard failures, and the `migrate-legacy-task.sh` script converts legacy markdown checklists into runnable eval stubs.\n152\t\n153\t### Phase 6 — Validate (pre-gate structural linter)\n154\t\n155\t> **Validate is NOT the gate.** It is the structural linter that runs BEFORE the gate. It does not execute evals; it does not stamp `signed_off`. See [concepts/signed-off.md](concepts/signed-off.md) for the validate-vs-gate contract.\n156\t\n157\tRun `taskspec validate tasks/T-*.md` (src/gate/validate-task-spec.sh):\n158\t- Confirms all 6 zones present\n159\t- Confirms all required frontmatter fields\n160\t- Confirms no `{{TODO}}` placeholders remain\n161\t- Confirms eval functions are syntactically valid bash\n162\t- Confirms `id` matches the filename basename\n163\t- Confirms every `depends_on` references an existing task (warns if target is parked/done)\n164\t- Confirms every `touches_paths` exists on disk (warns only if task is parked)\n165\t- Confirms Exit Check calls every `eval_N()` defined in Success Criteria\n166\t- For v2/v3 (which share the `agent_contract` v2 schema): confirms `agent_contract` has\n167\t  `produce` as YAML list, `emit` as enum list, `timeout_minutes` as 1–1440,\n168\t  `sandbox_type` as host|isolated|ephemeral, and `required_tools` as a non-empty list\n169\t- For v3 `profile: standard|full`: confirms a `## Behavior` section (Given/When/Then,\n170\t  `B-N` ids) exists and that every behavior is verified by ≥1 eval and every eval maps to\n171\t  a declared behavior (the behavior↔eval traceability lint)\n172\t- `--shellcheck-evals` (opt-in): pipes each `eval_N()` body through `shellcheck -S warning`\n173\t  to catch syntax errors and dead variables\n174\t- `--dry-run-eval` (opt-in): sources the bash blocks in a disposable subshell and verifies\n175\t  the Exit Check exits 0 against the current repo\n176\t\n177\t**State invariant:** Every successful validation idempotently updates `tasks/_state.yaml` with the\n178\t task's frontmatter snapshot, `last_validated` timestamp, and `validator_version`. Failed\n179\t validations do **not** write state — broken specs never pollute the audit trail. If\n180\t `_state.yaml` doesn't reflect a task file's frontmatter, the validator hasn't run on it\n181\t OR `rebuild-state.sh` is overdue.\n182\t\n183\t### Phase 7 — Gate (the autonomy contract)\n184\t\n185\t**This is THE gate.** It is the only path to `signed_off: true`. Authors do not hand-edit `signed_off`; hand-stamping is rejected by the v2.1 structural sign-off envelope check.\n186\t\n187\tRun `taskspec gate --stamp tasks/T-*.md` (src/gate/safe-to-delegate.sh):\n188\t\n189\t- Composes `validate-task-spec.sh` (structural) + `run-task-spec.sh` (eval execution) into one go/no-go verdict\n190\t- `validate` must exit 0 (structural pass)\n191\t- Eval bodies must execute without bash-level errors (no `syntax error`, no `unbound variable`, no `command not found`, no inverted-grep-c footguns)\n192\t- Assertion failures are EXPECTED on an unbuilt task — the work isn't done yet. The gate fails for the RIGHT reason (assertion not yet true) not the WRONG reason (eval is broken bash)\n193\t- On success: flips `signed_off: false` → `signed_off: true`, stamps `signed_off_by: $USER` (override with `--stamp-by`), and records `signed_off_at: <iso-8601>`\n194\t- On failure: prints `VERDICT: DO NOT DELEGATE` and exits 1. The spec stays `signed_off: false`\n195\t\n196\t`taskspec run` (src/gate/run-task-spec.sh) runs the evals directly (used by the gate; usually not invoked standalone):\n197\t\n198\t- Extracts Success Criteria and Exit Check bash blocks\n199\t- Runs each `eval_N()` in an isolated subshell with `set -euo pipefail`\n200\t- Captures per-eval stdout/stderr/duration\n201\t- Reports `pass`/`fail` per eval with timing\n202\t- Exits 0 only when the Exit Check returns 0\n203\t- `--ci` flag emits one JSON line per eval for non-interactive pipelines\n204\t\n205\t### Phase 8 — Dispatch\n206\t\n207\tA spec with `signed_off: true` is ready to hand to an autonomous executor. See [runbooks/dispatching-a-task-spec.md](runbooks/dispatching-a-task-spec.md) for the full handoff protocol. *Optional debrief:* **`pass-to-lesson`** teaches the signed-off backlog — every field, the decision it encodes, what breaks downstream without it — before dispatch.\n208\t\n209\tThe spec's frontmatter carries an `execution_backend:` field that names the canonical\n210\texecutor. **It is an OPEN STRING, not a closed enum** (validator treats any single token\n211\tas valid; see src/gate/validate-task-spec.sh): `any` (the default) defers to the author's\n212\tchoice, and any other token names a specific executor. The bundled per-engine dispatch\n213\tadapters are the **non-normative** layer — they live in `../harness/engines/` and\n214\teach carries its own dispatch command:\n215\t\n216\t| `execution_backend` value | Where to find the dispatch command |\n217\t|---------------------------|------------------------------------|\n218\t| `any` (default) | Author's choice — pick any conformant executor |\n219\t| named token (e.g. `claude`, `codex`, `kimi`, `cursor`, `taskship`, `anthive`) | the matching recipe in `../harness/engines/` |\n220\t| a token with no bundled recipe | `../harness/engines/custom.md` (DIY adapter) |\n221\t\n222\tReport to user:\n223\t\n224\t- File path + signed_off status\n225\t- Gate verdict (DELEGATE / DO NOT DELEGATE)\n226\t- Recommended dispatch command per `execution_backend`\n227\t- Optional: dispatch immediately if user said \"and run it\"\n228\t\n229\t---\n230\t\n231\t## Batch Sprint Compose (Fast Batch)\n232\t\n233\tUse the **Fast Batch** path when you're decomposing a known audit or feature\n234\tinto many atomic specs against an in-repo codebase. Skip the per-task MCP\n235\tresearch and architect spawn; compose directly from structured intent.\n236\t\n237\t### When to use Fast Batch vs Full Workflow\n238\t\n239\t| Criterion | Fast Batch | Full Workflow |\n240\t|-----------|------------|---------------|\n241\t| Domain familiarity | Known — you already understand the codebase | Unknown — greenfield task, unfamiliar library |\n242\t| MCP research value | Low — no external docs needed | High — need Context7 / Exa / Ref for failure modes |\n243\t| Intent structure | User provides `slug: description` per line | User gives a fuzzy paragraph |\n244\t| Task count | 3–20 specs at once | 1 spec at a time |\n245\t| Structural confidence | ≥ 0.95 — user already provided `touches_paths`, `evals`, etc. | < 0.95 — need task-architect to draft evals |\n246\t| Empirical signal | Prior batch validated first-try | Novel task with unknown failure modes |\n247\t\n248\t**Decision rule:** If ≥ 4 of the 6 criteria above favor Fast Batch, use it.\n249\tOtherwise, run the full 6-phase workflow.\n250\t\n251\t### Batch Sprint flow\n252\t\n253\t```text\n254\t┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n255\t│  BATCH SPRINT COMPOSE                                             │\n256\t├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n257\t│  Step 1: PREPARE        collect intent list (slug + description)  │\n258\t│  Step 2: GENERATE       batch-generate.sh → N stub T-*.md files   │\n259\t│  Step 3: FILL           edit stubs: title, why, goal, evals, etc. │\n260\t│  Step 4: BULK-VALIDATE  validate-task-spec.sh on each file        │\n261\t│  Step 5: COMMIT         git add tasks/ + state + metrics          │\n262\t└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n263\t```\n264\t\n265\t### Input format\n266\t\n267\tOne line per task in `slug: description` form:\n268\t\n269\t```text\n270\tfix-login-redirect   Redirect to /dashboard after OAuth success instead of /\n271\tadd-rate-limiting    Add 100 req/min rate limit to /api/v1/analyze endpoint\n272\tupdate-telemetry     Emit batch-completed event to Langfuse after each sprint\n273\t```\n274\t\n275\tSave to a file (e.g. `intents.txt`) and run:\n276\t\n277\t```bash\n278\ttaskspec batch \\\n279\t    --intent-file intents.txt \\\n280\t    --effort S \\\n281\t    --agent any \\\n282\t    --queue\n283\t```\n284\t\n285\tFlags:\n286\t- `--intent-file <path>` — required. File with one `slug: description` per line.\n287\t- `--effort S|M` — required. Applied to every generated spec.\n288\t- `--agent <name>` — optional. Defaults to `any`.\n289\t- `--source-note <path>` — optional. Applied to every spec.\n290\t- `--queue` — optional. Write to `tasks/queue/` instead of `tasks/`.\n291\t- `--dry-run` — optional. Print what would be created without writing files.\n292\t- `--skip-validation` — optional. Skip the bulk validation pass.\n293\t- `--validate-opts <opts>` — optional. Pass extra flags to the validator (e.g. `--skip-touches-paths`).\n294\t\n295\t### After generation\n296\t\n297\t1. **Fill each stub** — edit titles, `why`, `goal`, `touches_paths`, evals, anti-patterns, do-not-touch.\n298\t2. **Run bulk validation** — the script validates each file by default; failures are reported per-task.\n299\t3. **Commit** — `git add tasks/ tasks/_state.yaml tasks/_metrics.jsonl`\n300\t\n301\t### Anti-pattern: silent skipping\n302\t\n303\tDon't skip Phase 2 or Phase 4 silently. The `--fast` path is an **explicit opt-in**\n304\t(via `--intent-file` + known-domain context). If you're unsure, run the full\n305\tworkflow on one spec first, then batch the rest once the pattern is proven.\n306\t\n307\t---\n308\t\n309\t## Decomposition (intent → atoms)\n310\t\n311\tWhen the input is too big for one atomic spec — a PRD, a design doc, a fuzzy\n312\tintent, or \"a set of calls\" — **decompose it** into N linked atoms before\n313\tauthoring. The converged shape is a **flat parent index of stubs + per-task\n314\tdetail specs** (not a tree): `task-architect` finds the atoms, you wire the\n315\t`depends_on:` edges and the `parent:` field, and `batch-generate.sh` makes the\n316\tdetail stubs (this is fan-out *into* Batch Sprint Compose, not a replacement).\n317\t\n318\t**Holes are first-class blockers.** An unresolved open question is not a footnote:\n319\tan atom with an open hole is NOT safe-to-delegate. Encode the hole as\n320\t`status: blocked` + `blocked_reason:` (the validator maps `blocked` → A2A\n321\t`input-required` via `ts_a2a_state()`) plus a non-`(none)` `## Open Questions`\n322\tzone. A `blocked` atom is not `ready`, so it never reaches the safe-to-delegate\n323\tgate until the question is answered and it transitions to `ready`.\n324\t\n325\t**Quiz the user before generating stubs.** Present the proposed breakdown as a\n326\tnumbered list — per atom: title, `depends_on` edges, and what it delivers\n327\tend-to-end — and ask three things: does the granularity feel right (too\n328\tcoarse / too fine)? are the edges correct (each atom depends only on atoms\n329\tthat genuinely gate it)? should any be merged or split? Iterate until approved,\n330\t*then* run `batch-generate.sh`. Regenerating stubs is cheap; re-cutting a\n331\thalf-built backlog is not.\n332\t\n333\t**Wide refactors are the exception to vertical slicing.** A **wide refactor**\n334\tis one mechanical change — rename a column, retype a shared symbol — whose\n335\tblast radius fans across the whole codebase, so no vertical slice can land\n336\tgreen on its own. Don't force it into a tracer bullet; sequence it as\n337\t**expand–contract** atoms:\n338\t\n339\t1. **Expand** — add the new form beside the old so nothing breaks (one atom).\n340\t2. **Migrate** — move call sites over in batches sized by blast radius (per\n341\t   package, per directory), each batch its own atom with `depends_on: [expand]`,\n342\t   staying green because the old form still exists.\n343\t3. **Contract** — delete the old form once no caller remains (one atom,\n344\t   `depends_on` every migrate batch).\n345\t\n346\tEach atom still carries its own eval; the shape of the cut is what changes.\n347\t\n348\tSee [runbooks/decomposing-intent.md](runbooks/decomposing-intent.md) for the\n349\tstep-by-step method and [concepts/decomposition.md](concepts/decomposition.md)\n350\tfor the concept.\n351\t\n352\t---\n353\t\n354\t## Self-containment\n355\t\n356\tThe engine is portable as a single repo:\n357\t\n358\t```bash\n359\tgit clone https://github.com/luanmorenommaciel/task-spec.git\n360\tln -s \"$PWD/task-spec/bin/taskspec\" /usr/local/bin/taskspec\n361\t# optional: bash task-spec/src/lib/install.sh --target /path/to/repo\n362\t# taskspec now works in EVERY repo on that machine\n363\t```\n364\t\n365\t**Bash portability:** The core gate path — `validate-task-spec.sh`,\n366\t`safe-to-delegate.sh`, `run-task-spec.sh`, and `_lib.sh` (under `src/gate/` +\n367\t`src/lib/`) — is bash-3.2-safe and runs on the macOS system `/bin/bash`\n368\t(3.2.57) with no extra setup. Two auxiliary scripts, `lint-backlog.sh` and\n369\t`query-metrics.sh` (`src/backlog/`), use associative arrays (`declare -A`) and\n370\tso require **bash 4+**. They guard themselves via `ts_require_bash4`: if\n371\tlaunched under bash 3.x they auto-detect a bash 4+ on `PATH` and re-exec under\n372\tit, and if none is found they print a one-line remediation (`brew install\n373\tbash`) and exit 3 — never the cryptic `declare: -A: invalid option` failure.\n374\t\n375\tExternal dependencies: Context7, Exa, Ref MCPs must be user-configured.\n376\t\n377\t---\n378\t\n379\t## Worker layer (MCPs)\n380\t\n381\t| MCP | Purpose |\n382\t|-----|---------|\n383\t| Context7 | Fresh library/framework docs |\n384\t| Exa | Production examples and patterns |\n385\t| Ref | Canonical references |\n386\t\n387\t---\n388\t\n389\t## Specialist agent\n390\t\n391\tSpawns **`task-architect`** for the judgment-heavy work:\n392\t- Lives at: `~/.claude/agents/task-architect.md`\n393\t- Installed via: `bash src/lib/install.sh`\n394\t- Runs in: isolated context, returns structured build plan\n395\t- Scoped tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash, Write, TodoWrite, MCP suite\n396\t\n397\t---\n398\t\n399\t## Threshold Mapping (severity-scaled)\n400\t\n401\tTask quality threshold scales with consequence. Below the severity-specific\n402\tthreshold, the agent ASKs the user before composing. Above it, the agent\n403\tPROCEEDS with a disclaimer.\n404\t\n405\t| Severity | Threshold | Rationale |\n406\t|----------|----------:|-----------|\n407\t| cosmetic | 0.80 | Doc typos, comment fixes; cheap to revert |\n408\t| refactor | 0.85 | Code shape changes with tests; semantic equivalence required |\n409\t| feature | 0.90 | New behavior; well-scoped acceptance criteria |\n410\t| bugfix | 0.95 | Correctness change with regression risk (legacy default) |\n411\t| security | 0.98 | Authentication, auth, cryptography, secrets handling |\n412\t| financial-critical | 0.99 | Money fields, accounting, ledger; silent errors compound |\n413\t\n414\t**Backward compatibility:** Tasks without a `severity` field default to `bugfix`\n415\t(0.95), preserving the historical IMPORTANT threshold.\n416\t\n417\t| Action | Class |\n418\t|--------|-------|\n419\t| Reading the user's intent | ADVISORY |\n420\t| MCP research | STANDARD |\n421\t| Drafting evals | IMPORTANT (severity-scaled) |\n422\t| Refusing direct dispatch of XL/XXL nodes, or L on an ineligible backend | CRITICAL |\n423\t| Detecting subjective output | CRITICAL (refuses EDD, routes to SDD) |\n424\t\n425\t---\n426\t\n427\t## CLI commands (bundled under src/, dispatched by bin/taskspec)\n428\t\n429\t| Command | Purpose |\n430\t|---------|---------|\n431\t| `generate-task-spec.sh <slug> <effort> [agent] [source]` | Create a new T-*.md from template |\n432\t| `batch-generate.sh --intent-file <path> --effort S|M [opts]` | Bulk-create N stub T-*.md files from an intent list, then bulk-validate |\n433\t| `validate-task-spec.sh [opts] <path>` | Lint a T-*.md against the current v3 format (legacy v0/v1/v2 accepted with warnings). Flags: `--shellcheck-evals`, `--dry-run-eval`, `--skip-touches-paths`, `--strict-depends`. `--emit-schema {frontmatter\\|agent-contract}` prints the published JSON Schema (Draft 2020-12) and exits |\n434\t| `lint-backlog.sh [--help]` | Cross-task linter: detects touches_paths overlaps, depends_on cycles, duplicate IDs, stale preconditions across the backlog |\n435\t| `run-task-spec.sh [--ci] <path>` | Execute evals from a T-*.md. Runs each `eval_N` in isolation, then the Exit Check. `--ci` emits JSON-per-eval |\n436\t| `safe-to-delegate.sh [--stamp] [--skip-touches-paths] <path>` | **PRE-delegation gate.** Structural validate + shellcheck-evals + eval execution (broken-logic guard). Emits DELEGATE / DO-NOT-DELEGATE (exit 0/1). Asks \"are the evals well-formed enough to delegate?\" — assertion failure is EXPECTED for unbuilt work. |\n437\t| `accept-task.sh --handoff <file> [--stamp] [--gold-sanity] <path>` | **POST-execution gate.** Re-runs evals, verifies HMAC v3/TaskRevision, dependency closure, immutable Git base, committed+index+worktree+untracked blast radius, and required receipt policy. On `--stamp`, writes `AcceptanceRecord/v1` and the complete acceptance envelope atomically. `--no-blast-radius`, a missing key, a narrow signature, or a legacy receipt forces explicit supervised Tier 2. `--gold-sanity` blocks non-discriminating evals that also pass on the unpatched baseline. |\n438\t| `conformance-check.sh --level L0\\|L1\\|L2 --executor \"<cmd>\"` | **Executor conformance suite (v3).** Certifies that a CONSUMER honors the contract (reads format + runs evals / lifecycle / budget+park). `--self-test` runs the bundled `ref-executor.sh`. Makes \"any conformant executor can pick it up\" testable. |\n439\t| `ref-executor.sh <path>` | The canonical L2-conformant reference EXECUTOR (~60 lines). Not an AI agent — the worked example of the consumer contract (acquire lock → iterate within budget → done/park) for adapter authors. |\n440\t| `migrate-legacy-task.sh <path>` | Convert a legacy task's markdown checklist into v2.1 eval stubs + validation_card |\n441\t| `transition-status.sh <id> <new-status> [reason]` | Atomic status change |\n442\t| `rebuild-state.sh` | Regenerate `_state.yaml` from frontmatter (recovery). Run after direct writes or when state is stale. |\n443\t| `list-ready.sh [--effort=S] [--agent=any]` | Show tasks ready for pickup |\n444\t| `archive.sh` | Move done/parked tasks to subdirs |\n445\t| `backup-backlog.sh [dir]` | Snapshot tasks/ folder |\n446\t| `install-hooks.sh` | Install git pre-commit hook that runs `rebuild-state.sh` to catch drift from direct-Write users |\n447\t| `install.sh` | Install bundled agent to ~/.claude/agents/ |\n448\t\n449\t---\n450\t\n451\t## Linting\n452\t\n453\tTask-Spec v3 files use YAML frontmatter with a `title:` field **and** a body H1 (`# Title`). This is intentional: the frontmatter title is machine-readable metadata; the body H1 is human-readable when the file is rendered outside a parser. The combination triggers **MD025** (multiple H1 headings) in markdownlint by default.\n454\t\n455\tThis skill ships a scoped `.markdownlintrc` in its root that tells markdownlint to treat the frontmatter `title:` as the document's sole H1:\n456\t\n457\t```json\n458\t{\n459\t  \"MD025\": {\n460\t    \"front_matter_title\": \"\"\n461\t  }\n462\t}\n463\t```\n464\t\n465\tThe empty string `\"\"` matches the frontmatter key name exactly, so the body H1 is no longer flagged as a duplicate.\n466\t\n467\t**How to use:**\n468\t- If your IDE or linter looks at the skill root, no action needed.\n469\t- If your IDE lints from `tasks/` or the repo root, copy or symlink the config:\n470\t  ```bash\n471\t  # ship your own .markdownlintrc at tasks/ (see the template in this doc)\n472\t  ```\n473\t\n474\t**Why not drop the body H1?** Because it breaks every existing spec and reduces readability for humans skimming raw markdown. The skill owns its convention via config rather than changing the convention.\n475\t\n476\t---\n477\t\n478\t## References\n479\t\n480\t- `../spec/task-spec-v3.md` — the published format-v3 specification and compatibility history\n481\t- `concepts/eval-driven-development.md` — EDD methodology\n482\t- `concepts/edd-vs-sdd-honest-comparison.md` — when to use which\n483\t- `concepts/six-zones.md` — zone-by-zone deep dive\n484\t- `concepts/profiles.md` — **v3** effort-scaled profiles (lite/standard/full) + the behavior↔eval traceability rule\n485\t- `concepts/conformance-levels.md` — **v3** executor conformance L0/L1/L2 + the A2A lifecycle mapping\n486\t- `concepts/effort-gate.md` — XS/S/M/L leaves, XL/XXL nodes, and capability-based L routing\n487\t- `concepts/agent-contract.md` — cross-vendor contract (includes the generic `backend_metadata` field; the backend names itself)\n488\t- `concepts/decomposition.md` — **v3** intent/PRD → N atomic specs (flat index + detail atoms, holes-as-blockers)\n489\t- `concepts/backlog-architecture.md` — 5-layer state management\n490\t- `../spec/schemas/` — published Task-Spec, TaskPlan, TaskHandoff, AuthoringEvidence, and agent-contract schemas\n491\t- `patterns/runnable-bash-evals.md` — eval writing patterns\n492\t- `patterns/validation-card-yaml.md` — YAML contract patterns\n493\t- `patterns/atomic-status-transitions.md` — transition protocol\n494\t- `patterns/anti-patterns-extraction.md` — mining from MCP research\n495\t- `patterns/do-not-touch-detection.md` — repo-scan patterns\n496\t- `runbooks/from-fuzzy-intent.md` — paragraph → Task-Spec\n497\t- `runbooks/decomposing-intent.md` — **v3** intent/PRD/set-of-calls → N linked atomic specs\n498\t- `runbooks/from-meeting-note.md` — Krisp output → Task-Spec\n499\t- `runbooks/from-existing-task.md` — legacy task → v2.1 conversion\n500\t- `runbooks/batch-sprint-compose.md` — bulk intent list → N Task-Spec stubs\n501\t- `runbooks/validating-a-task-spec.md` — linting walkthrough\n502\t- `runbooks/recovering-from-crash.md` — state recovery\n503\t- `runbooks/empirical-experiment-protocol.md` — SDD vs EDD test design\n504\t\n505\t---\n506\t\n507\t## Remember\n508\t\n509\t> **\"Specs that verify themselves keep humans at the edges of the loop — at\n510\t> intent-setting and acceptance review — not in the middle of every iteration.\"**\n511\t\n512\t**Mission:** Be the cornerstone primitive — the open, atomic, vendor-neutral,\n513\tself-verifying unit-of-work format that any conformant agentic system can produce,\n514\tconsume, and trust. Humans enter at intent and at acceptance review; the execution\n515\tloop in between runs without them.\n516\t\n517\t> **On trust:** the HMAC sign-off envelope is **tamper-evident, not tamper-proof**\n518\t> — it proves a spec's evals were not edited after the gate stamped them, with a\n519\t> repo-shared key. It is a drift/accidental-edit guard, not a security boundary.\n520\t> \"Humans at intent + review\" is the honest claim; \"no humans\" is not.\n521\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0abf1529-dfad-44b4-923a-1389e8368909","uuid":"06bc0a61-b151-41a7-bafa-d2c882ff1e20","timestamp":"2026-09-14T21:50:42.736Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat iw54pyk7/.agents/skills/task-spec/references/authoring-workflow.md","content":"# Authoring workflow — the task-spec doctrine\n\n> This is the **authoring doctrine** for the task-spec engine. The installer ships\n> it as `references/authoring-workflow.md` inside every installed skill. It is\n> invoked via the canonical root `SKILL.md`, the compatibility skill in\n> [`harness/claude-code/`](../harness/claude-code/SKILL.md), or by\n> calling the `taskspec` CLI directly. Script references below map to\n> `bin/taskspec` subcommands (`new`, `batch`, `validate`, `gate`, `run`,\n> `accept`, `transition`, `ready`, `rebuild-state`, `archive`, `backup`,\n> `lint`, `metrics`, `conformance`); script files live under `src/<verb>/`.\n\n\n> **Identity:** The open, atomic unit-of-work format for autonomous agentic systems\n> **Domain:** Task PRD generation, eval-driven development, backlog management\n> **Default Threshold:** severity-scaled (0.80–0.99) — see Threshold Mapping below\n\nThis skill is part of the **CAW Triad** for task generation:\n- **C** (this Skill): the workflow + bundled scripts + templates\n- **A** (Agent): `task-architect` — applies Agreement Matrix, ensures quality\n- **W** (MCPs): Context7, Exa, Ref — fresh validation at authoring time\n\n---\n\n## When to invoke this skill\n\nTrigger conditions (Claude auto-invokes when context matches):\n- User says \"create a task,\" \"scaffold a task,\" \"decompose this into work\"\n- User has fuzzy intent (\"verify the deploy pipeline works\")\n- User wants to convert a meeting note into actionable backlog\n- User mentions Task-Spec, EDD, eval-driven, or \"make this executable\"\n- User asks for a task that any conformant engine (e.g. Claude/Codex/Cursor) can pick up\n\nPause authoring if:\n- The outcome is still too fuzzy to declare behaviors and discriminating evals\n- An L leaf has multiple independent done-conditions (decompose it)\n- Subjective output has no reviewable machine or human oracle yet\n- User just wants a one-off prompt, not a reusable spec\n\n---\n\n## Workflow\n\n```text\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  TASK-SPEC WORKFLOW                                               │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  Phase 1: UNDERSTAND    parse intent, classify effort             │\n│  Phase 2: RESEARCH      optional AuthoringEvidence v1             │\n│  Phase 3: SCAN          read host repo for touches_paths          │\n│  Phase 4: ARCHITECT     spawn task-architect agent for judgment   │\n│  Phase 5: COMPOSE       TaskPlan preview, then declared specs     │\n│  Phase 6: VALIDATE      structural linter — validate-task-spec.sh │\n│  Phase 7: GATE          PRE-gate — safe-to-delegate.sh --stamp    │\n│  Phase 8: DISPATCH      hand off to executor per execution_backend│\n│  Phase 9: ACCEPT        POST-gate — accept-task.sh --stamp        │\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n> **The loop is closed (v3).** Phase 7 certifies the SPEC is delegate-safe (evals\n> well-formed; assertion failure expected). Phase 9 certifies the WORK is real\n> (evals now PASS from a clean checkout + change set within blast radius + sign-off\n> HMAC intact). A task is provably DONE only when `accept-task.sh` writes\n> `accepted: true`. Humans sit at Phase 1 (intent) and review the Phase 9 verdict —\n> not in the middle of the execution loop. See\n> [concepts/conformance-levels.md](concepts/conformance-levels.md).\n\n> **Alternative: Batch Sprint Compose** — when you need N specs at once in a\n> known domain, skip Phase 2 (MCP research) and Phase 4 (task-architect spawn).\n> See [Batch Sprint Compose](#batch-sprint-compose-fast-batch) below.\n\n### Phase 1 — Understand\n\nParse the user's intent. Critical questions answered before scaffolding:\n\n1. **What's the verbal description?** (1 paragraph from user)\n2. **Effort class?** XS/S/M/L are leaves; L needs one coherent done-condition and a configured long-horizon backend. XL/XXL are child-composing nodes, not executor work. See `concepts/effort-gate.md`.\n3. **Agent hint?** `any` (vendor-portable) OR specific (`python-developer`, etc.)\n4. **Source provenance?** Meeting note, audit, ticket — must have one\n5. **Touches what paths?** Best guess; refined in Phase 3\n\nIf unclear, ASK ONCE for clarification. Don't proceed with vague intent.\n\n### Phase 2 — Research (optional evidence)\n\nWhen current external evidence is necessary, explicitly select a provider and\nnormalize its output to `AuthoringEvidence/v1`:\n\n```text\nFirecrawl / Tavily / Exa / another explicit provider → bounded, cited evidence\n```\n\nThe research output feeds the `anti-patterns` and `do-not-touch` zones —\nboth should be informed by real-world failure modes from MCP findings.\n\n### Phase 3 — Scan\n\nRead the host repo to refine:\n- Which files actually exist on `touches_paths`?\n- Any cross-cutting concerns (touches the same files as in-flight tasks)?\n- Existing conventions (CLAUDE.md, .editorconfig, project style)?\n\nThis uses Glob + Grep + Read in the host repo — no writes.\n\n### Phase 4 — Architect (spawn the agent)\n\nSpawn the **`task-architect`** agent with:\n- The intent\n- Phase 2 research findings\n- Phase 3 repo scan findings\n\nThe agent:\n- Applies Agreement Matrix (KB + MCP)\n- Decides isolation class (worktree vs branch)\n- Drafts the 3 evals as runnable bash\n- Drafts anti-patterns + do-not-touch from research\n- Returns a structured build plan with confidence score\n\nIf confidence < 0.95, ASK the user before composing.\n\n### Phase 5 — Compose\n\nRun `taskspec new` (src/author/generate-task-spec.sh) to create the T-*.md file from the template,\nthen fill in:\n\n| Zone | What goes in |\n|------|--------------|\n| Frontmatter | id, title, status=ready, format_version=3, profile, effort, budget=15, agent, touches_paths, source_note |\n| Zone 1 | Why / Goal / Context (lean) |\n| Zone 2 | 3+ evals + validation_card YAML + exit_check bash |\n| Zone 3 | Rollback Plan (or `(none)`) |\n| Zone 4 | Observability Hooks (or `(none)`) |\n| Zone 5 | Anti-patterns + do-not-touch list |\n| Zone 6 | Open questions (or `(none)`) |\n\n**Two shape rules govern every atom:**\n\n- **Cut tracer bullets, not layers.** For feature work, prefer a **vertical\n  slice** — a narrow but COMPLETE path through every layer the feature\n  touches (schema → transform → serving → test), demoable or verifiable on\n  its own — over a horizontal slice of one layer. A horizontal atom (\"all the\n  models\", \"all the endpoints\") passes its own eval while proving nothing\n  end-to-end; a tracer bullet's eval exercises the seam the system actually\n  ships through. The eval defines *done*; the vertical cut defines *worth\n  doing alone*.\n- **Size to one fresh context window.** An atom must fit — spec, cited ADRs,\n  touched files, and the working diff — inside a single fresh executor\n  session. If holding the task means the executor must page out what it read,\n  the atom is too big: split it and wire `depends_on`. This is the practical\n  ceiling within each runnable XS/S/M/L leaf, not a new class.\n\n**Layered policy (legacy tolerance):** `format_version` is `3` for new specs (the template's default; see `src/templates/task-spec.md.tpl`). Tasks created before 2026-05-27 (or explicitly marked `format_version: 0`, `1`, or `2`) are treated as legacy. The validator accepts legacy v0/v1/v2 tasks with warnings rather than hard failures, and the `migrate-legacy-task.sh` script converts legacy markdown checklists into runnable eval stubs.\n\n### Phase 6 — Validate (pre-gate structural linter)\n\n> **Validate is NOT the gate.** It is the structural linter that runs BEFORE the gate. It does not execute evals; it does not stamp `signed_off`. See [concepts/signed-off.md](concepts/signed-off.md) for the validate-vs-gate contract.\n\nRun `taskspec validate tasks/T-*.md` (src/gate/validate-task-spec.sh):\n- Confirms all 6 zones present\n- Confirms all required frontmatter fields\n- Confirms no `{{TODO}}` placeholders remain\n- Confirms eval functions are syntactically valid bash\n- Confirms `id` matches the filename basename\n- Confirms every `depends_on` references an existing task (warns if target is parked/done)\n- Confirms every `touches_paths` exists on disk (warns only if task is parked)\n- Confirms Exit Check calls every `eval_N()` defined in Success Criteria\n- For v2/v3 (which share the `agent_contract` v2 schema): confirms `agent_contract` has\n  `produce` as YAML list, `emit` as enum list, `timeout_minutes` as 1–1440,\n  `sandbox_type` as host|isolated|ephemeral, and `required_tools` as a non-empty list\n- For v3 `profile: standard|full`: confirms a `## Behavior` section (Given/When/Then,\n  `B-N` ids) exists and that every behavior is verified by ≥1 eval and every eval maps to\n  a declared behavior (the behavior↔eval traceability lint)\n- `--shellcheck-evals` (opt-in): pipes each `eval_N()` body through `shellcheck -S warning`\n  to catch syntax errors and dead variables\n- `--dry-run-eval` (opt-in): sources the bash blocks in a disposable subshell and verifies\n  the Exit Check exits 0 against the current repo\n\n**State invariant:** Every successful validation idempotently updates `tasks/_state.yaml` with the\n task's frontmatter snapshot, `last_validated` timestamp, and `validator_version`. Failed\n validations do **not** write state — broken specs never pollute the audit trail. If\n `_state.yaml` doesn't reflect a task file's frontmatter, the validator hasn't run on it\n OR `rebuild-state.sh` is overdue.\n\n### Phase 7 — Gate (the autonomy contract)\n\n**This is THE gate.** It is the only path to `signed_off: true`. Authors do not hand-edit `signed_off`; hand-stamping is rejected by the v2.1 structural sign-off envelope check.\n\nRun `taskspec gate --stamp tasks/T-*.md` (src/gate/safe-to-delegate.sh):\n\n- Composes `validate-task-spec.sh` (structural) + `run-task-spec.sh` (eval execution) into one go/no-go verdict\n- `validate` must exit 0 (structural pass)\n- Eval bodies must execute without bash-level errors (no `syntax error`, no `unbound variable`, no `command not found`, no inverted-grep-c footguns)\n- Assertion failures are EXPECTED on an unbuilt task — the work isn't done yet. The gate fails for the RIGHT reason (assertion not yet true) not the WRONG reason (eval is broken bash)\n- On success: flips `signed_off: false` → `signed_off: true`, stamps `signed_off_by: $USER` (override with `--stamp-by`), and records `signed_off_at: <iso-8601>`\n- On failure: prints `VERDICT: DO NOT DELEGATE` and exits 1. The spec stays `signed_off: false`\n\n`taskspec run` (src/gate/run-task-spec.sh) runs the evals directly (used by the gate; usually not invoked standalone):\n\n- Extracts Success Criteria and Exit Check bash blocks\n- Runs each `eval_N()` in an isolated subshell with `set -euo pipefail`\n- Captures per-eval stdout/stderr/duration\n- Reports `pass`/`fail` per eval with timing\n- Exits 0 only when the Exit Check returns 0\n- `--ci` flag emits one JSON line per eval for non-interactive pipelines\n\n### Phase 8 — Dispatch\n\nA spec with `signed_off: true` is ready to hand to an autonomous executor. See [runbooks/dispatching-a-task-spec.md](runbooks/dispatching-a-task-spec.md) for the full handoff protocol. *Optional debrief:* **`pass-to-lesson`** teaches the signed-off backlog — every field, the decision it encodes, what breaks downstream without it — before dispatch.\n\nThe spec's frontmatter carries an `execution_backend:` field that names the canonical\nexecutor. **It is an OPEN STRING, not a closed enum** (validator treats any single token\nas valid; see src/gate/validate-task-spec.sh): `any` (the default) defers to the author's\nchoice, and any other token names a specific executor. The bundled per-engine dispatch\nadapters are the **non-normative** layer — they live in `../harness/engines/` and\neach carries its own dispatch command:\n\n| `execution_backend` value | Where to find the dispatch command |\n|---------------------------|------------------------------------|\n| `any` (default) | Author's choice — pick any conformant executor |\n| named token (e.g. `claude`, `codex`, `kimi`, `cursor`, `taskship`, `anthive`) | the matching recipe in `../harness/engines/` |\n| a token with no bundled recipe | `../harness/engines/custom.md` (DIY adapter) |\n\nReport to user:\n\n- File path + signed_off status\n- Gate verdict (DELEGATE / DO NOT DELEGATE)\n- Recommended dispatch command per `execution_backend`\n- Optional: dispatch immediately if user said \"and run it\"\n\n---\n\n## Batch Sprint Compose (Fast Batch)\n\nUse the **Fast Batch** path when you're decomposing a known audit or feature\ninto many atomic specs against an in-repo codebase. Skip the per-task MCP\nresearch and architect spawn; compose directly from structured intent.\n\n### When to use Fast Batch vs Full Workflow\n\n| Criterion | Fast Batch | Full Workflow |\n|-----------|------------|---------------|\n| Domain familiarity | Known — you already understand the codebase | Unknown — greenfield task, unfamiliar library |\n| MCP research value | Low — no external docs needed | High — need Context7 / Exa / Ref for failure modes |\n| Intent structure | User provides `slug: description` per line | User gives a fuzzy paragraph |\n| Task count | 3–20 specs at once | 1 spec at a time |\n| Structural confidence | ≥ 0.95 — user already provided `touches_paths`, `evals`, etc. | < 0.95 — need task-architect to draft evals |\n| Empirical signal | Prior batch validated first-try | Novel task with unknown failure modes |\n\n**Decision rule:** If ≥ 4 of the 6 criteria above favor Fast Batch, use it.\nOtherwise, run the full 6-phase workflow.\n\n### Batch Sprint flow\n\n```text\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  BATCH SPRINT COMPOSE                                             │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  Step 1: PREPARE        collect intent list (slug + description)  │\n│  Step 2: GENERATE       batch-generate.sh → N stub T-*.md files   │\n│  Step 3: FILL           edit stubs: title, why, goal, evals, etc. │\n│  Step 4: BULK-VALIDATE  validate-task-spec.sh on each file        │\n│  Step 5: COMMIT         git add tasks/ + state + metrics          │\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Input format\n\nOne line per task in `slug: description` form:\n\n```text\nfix-login-redirect   Redirect to /dashboard after OAuth success instead of /\nadd-rate-limiting    Add 100 req/min rate limit to /api/v1/analyze endpoint\nupdate-telemetry     Emit batch-completed event to Langfuse after each sprint\n```\n\nSave to a file (e.g. `intents.txt`) and run:\n\n```bash\ntaskspec batch \\\n    --intent-file intents.txt \\\n    --effort S \\\n    --agent any \\\n    --queue\n```\n\nFlags:\n- `--intent-file <path>` — required. File with one `slug: description` per line.\n- `--effort S|M` — required. Applied to every generated spec.\n- `--agent <name>` — optional. Defaults to `any`.\n- `--source-note <path>` — optional. Applied to every spec.\n- `--queue` — optional. Write to `tasks/queue/` instead of `tasks/`.\n- `--dry-run` — optional. Print what would be created without writing files.\n- `--skip-validation` — optional. Skip the bulk validation pass.\n- `--validate-opts <opts>` — optional. Pass extra flags to the validator (e.g. `--skip-touches-paths`).\n\n### After generation\n\n1. **Fill each stub** — edit titles, `why`, `goal`, `touches_paths`, evals, anti-patterns, do-not-touch.\n2. **Run bulk validation** — the script validates each file by default; failures are reported per-task.\n3. **Commit** — `git add tasks/ tasks/_state.yaml tasks/_metrics.jsonl`\n\n### Anti-pattern: silent skipping\n\nDon't skip Phase 2 or Phase 4 silently. The `--fast` path is an **explicit opt-in**\n(via `--intent-file` + known-domain context). If you're unsure, run the full\nworkflow on one spec first, then batch the rest once the pattern is proven.\n\n---\n\n## Decomposition (intent → atoms)\n\nWhen the input is too big for one atomic spec — a PRD, a design doc, a fuzzy\nintent, or \"a set of calls\" — **decompose it** into N linked atoms before\nauthoring. The converged shape is a **flat parent index of stubs + per-task\ndetail specs** (not a tree): `task-architect` finds the atoms, you wire the\n`depends_on:` edges and the `parent:` field, and `batch-generate.sh` makes the\ndetail stubs (this is fan-out *into* Batch Sprint Compose, not a replacement).\n\n**Holes are first-class blockers.** An unresolved open question is not a footnote:\nan atom with an open hole is NOT safe-to-delegate. Encode the hole as\n`status: blocked` + `blocked_reason:` (the validator maps `blocked` → A2A\n`input-required` via `ts_a2a_state()`) plus a non-`(none)` `## Open Questions`\nzone. A `blocked` atom is not `ready`, so it never reaches the safe-to-delegate\ngate until the question is answered and it transitions to `ready`.\n\n**Quiz the user before generating stubs.** Present the proposed breakdown as a\nnumbered list — per atom: title, `depends_on` edges, and what it delivers\nend-to-end — and ask three things: does the granularity feel right (too\ncoarse / too fine)? are the edges correct (each atom depends only on atoms\nthat genuinely gate it)? should any be merged or split? Iterate until approved,\n*then* run `batch-generate.sh`. Regenerating stubs is cheap; re-cutting a\nhalf-built backlog is not.\n\n**Wide refactors are the exception to vertical slicing.** A **wide refactor**\nis one mechanical change — rename a column, retype a shared symbol — whose\nblast radius fans across the whole codebase, so no vertical slice can land\ngreen on its own. Don't force it into a tracer bullet; sequence it as\n**expand–contract** atoms:\n\n1. **Expand** — add the new form beside the old so nothing breaks (one atom).\n2. **Migrate** — move call sites over in batches sized by blast radius (per\n   package, per directory), each batch its own atom with `depends_on: [expand]`,\n   staying green because the old form still exists.\n3. **Contract** — delete the old form once no caller remains (one atom,\n   `depends_on` every migrate batch).\n\nEach atom still carries its own eval; the shape of the cut is what changes.\n\nSee [runbooks/decomposing-intent.md](runbooks/decomposing-intent.md) for the\nstep-by-step method and [concepts/decomposition.md](concepts/decomposition.md)\nfor the concept.\n\n---\n\n## Self-containment\n\nThe engine is portable as a single repo:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/luanmorenommaciel/task-spec.git\nln -s \"$PWD/task-spec/bin/taskspec\" /usr/local/bin/taskspec\n# optional: bash task-spec/src/lib/install.sh --target /path/to/repo\n# taskspec now works in EVERY repo on that machine\n```\n\n**Bash portability:** The core gate path — `validate-task-spec.sh`,\n`safe-to-delegate.sh`, `run-task-spec.sh`, and `_lib.sh` (under `src/gate/` +\n`src/lib/`) — is bash-3.2-safe and runs on the macOS system `/bin/bash`\n(3.2.57) with no extra setup. Two auxiliary scripts, `lint-backlog.sh` and\n`query-metrics.sh` (`src/backlog/`), use associative arrays (`declare -A`) and\nso require **bash 4+**. They guard themselves via `ts_require_bash4`: if\nlaunched under bash 3.x they auto-detect a bash 4+ on `PATH` and re-exec under\nit, and if none is found they print a one-line remediation (`brew install\nbash`) and exit 3 — never the cryptic `declare: -A: invalid option` failure.\n\nExternal dependencies: Context7, Exa, Ref MCPs must be user-configured.\n\n---\n\n## Worker layer (MCPs)\n\n| MCP | Purpose |\n|-----|---------|\n| Context7 | Fresh library/framework docs |\n| Exa | Production examples and patterns |\n| Ref | Canonical references |\n\n---\n\n## Specialist agent\n\nSpawns **`task-architect`** for the judgment-heavy work:\n- Lives at: `~/.claude/agents/task-architect.md`\n- Installed via: `bash src/lib/install.sh`\n- Runs in: isolated context, returns structured build plan\n- Scoped tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash, Write, TodoWrite, MCP suite\n\n---\n\n## Threshold Mapping (severity-scaled)\n\nTask quality threshold scales with consequence. Below the severity-specific\nthreshold, the agent ASKs the user before composing. Above it, the agent\nPROCEEDS with a disclaimer.\n\n| Severity | Threshold | Rationale |\n|----------|----------:|-----------|\n| cosmetic | 0.80 | Doc typos, comment fixes; cheap to revert |\n| refactor | 0.85 | Code shape changes with tests; semantic equivalence required |\n| feature | 0.90 | New behavior; well-scoped acceptance criteria |\n| bugfix | 0.95 | Correctness change with regression risk (legacy default) |\n| security | 0.98 | Authentication, auth, cryptography, secrets handling |\n| financial-critical | 0.99 | Money fields, accounting, ledger; silent errors compound |\n\n**Backward compatibility:** Tasks without a `severity` field default to `bugfix`\n(0.95), preserving the historical IMPORTANT threshold.\n\n| Action | Class |\n|--------|-------|\n| Reading the user's intent | ADVISORY |\n| MCP research | STANDARD |\n| Drafting evals | IMPORTANT (severity-scaled) |\n| Refusing direct dispatch of XL/XXL nodes, or L on an ineligible backend | CRITICAL |\n| Detecting subjective output | CRITICAL (refuses EDD, routes to SDD) |\n\n---\n\n## CLI commands (bundled under src/, dispatched by bin/taskspec)\n\n| Command | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| `generate-task-spec.sh <slug> <effort> [agent] [source]` | Create a new T-*.md from template |\n| `batch-generate.sh --intent-file <path> --effort S|M [opts]` | Bulk-create N stub T-*.md files from an intent list, then bulk-validate |\n| `validate-task-spec.sh [opts] <path>` | Lint a T-*.md against the current v3 format (legacy v0/v1/v2 accepted with warnings). Flags: `--shellcheck-evals`, `--dry-run-eval`, `--skip-touches-paths`, `--strict-depends`. `--emit-schema {frontmatter\\|agent-contract}` prints the published JSON Schema (Draft 2020-12) and exits |\n| `lint-backlog.sh [--help]` | Cross-task linter: detects touches_paths overlaps, depends_on cycles, duplicate IDs, stale preconditions across the backlog |\n| `run-task-spec.sh [--ci] <path>` | Execute evals from a T-*.md. Runs each `eval_N` in isolation, then the Exit Check. `--ci` emits JSON-per-eval |\n| `safe-to-delegate.sh [--stamp] [--skip-touches-paths] <path>` | **PRE-delegation gate.** Structural validate + shellcheck-evals + eval execution (broken-logic guard). Emits DELEGATE / DO-NOT-DELEGATE (exit 0/1). Asks \"are the evals well-formed enough to delegate?\" — assertion failure is EXPECTED for unbuilt work. |\n| `accept-task.sh --handoff <file> [--stamp] [--gold-sanity] <path>` | **POST-execution gate.** Re-runs evals, verifies HMAC v3/TaskRevision, dependency closure, immutable Git base, committed+index+worktree+untracked blast radius, and required receipt policy. On `--stamp`, writes `AcceptanceRecord/v1` and the complete acceptance envelope atomically. `--no-blast-radius`, a missing key, a narrow signature, or a legacy receipt forces explicit supervised Tier 2. `--gold-sanity` blocks non-discriminating evals that also pass on the unpatched baseline. |\n| `conformance-check.sh --level L0\\|L1\\|L2 --executor \"<cmd>\"` | **Executor conformance suite (v3).** Certifies that a CONSUMER honors the contract (reads format + runs evals / lifecycle / budget+park). `--self-test` runs the bundled `ref-executor.sh`. Makes \"any conformant executor can pick it up\" testable. |\n| `ref-executor.sh <path>` | The canonical L2-conformant reference EXECUTOR (~60 lines). Not an AI agent — the worked example of the consumer contract (acquire lock → iterate within budget → done/park) for adapter authors. |\n| `migrate-legacy-task.sh <path>` | Convert a legacy task's markdown checklist into v2.1 eval stubs + validation_card |\n| `transition-status.sh <id> <new-status> [reason]` | Atomic status change |\n| `rebuild-state.sh` | Regenerate `_state.yaml` from frontmatter (recovery). Run after direct writes or when state is stale. |\n| `list-ready.sh [--effort=S] [--agent=any]` | Show tasks ready for pickup |\n| `archive.sh` | Move done/parked tasks to subdirs |\n| `backup-backlog.sh [dir]` | Snapshot tasks/ folder |\n| `install-hooks.sh` | Install git pre-commit hook that runs `rebuild-state.sh` to catch drift from direct-Write users |\n| `install.sh` | Install bundled agent to ~/.claude/agents/ |\n\n---\n\n## Linting\n\nTask-Spec v3 files use YAML frontmatter with a `title:` field **and** a body H1 (`# Title`). This is intentional: the frontmatter title is machine-readable metadata; the body H1 is human-readable when the file is rendered outside a parser. The combination triggers **MD025** (multiple H1 headings) in markdownlint by default.\n\nThis skill ships a scoped `.markdownlintrc` in its root that tells markdownlint to treat the frontmatter `title:` as the document's sole H1:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"MD025\": {\n    \"front_matter_title\": \"\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe empty string `\"\"` matches the frontmatter key name exactly, so the body H1 is no longer flagged as a duplicate.\n\n**How to use:**\n- If your IDE or linter looks at the skill root, no action needed.\n- If your IDE lints from `tasks/` or the repo root, copy or symlink the config:\n  ```bash\n  # ship your own .markdownlintrc at tasks/ (see the template in this doc)\n  ```\n\n**Why not drop the body H1?** Because it breaks every existing spec and reduces readability for humans skimming raw markdown. The skill owns its convention via config rather than changing the convention.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n- `../spec/task-spec-v3.md` — the published format-v3 specification and compatibility history\n- `concepts/eval-driven-development.md` — EDD methodology\n- `concepts/edd-vs-sdd-honest-comparison.md` — when to use which\n- `concepts/six-zones.md` — zone-by-zone deep dive\n- `concepts/profiles.md` — **v3** effort-scaled profiles (lite/standard/full) + the behavior↔eval traceability rule\n- `concepts/conformance-levels.md` — **v3** executor conformance L0/L1/L2 + the A2A lifecycle mapping\n- `concepts/effort-gate.md` — XS/S/M/L leaves, XL/XXL nodes, and capability-based L routing\n- `concepts/agent-contract.md` — cross-vendor contract (includes the generic `backend_metadata` field; the backend names itself)\n- `concepts/decomposition.md` — **v3** intent/PRD → N atomic specs (flat index + detail atoms, holes-as-blockers)\n- `concepts/backlog-architecture.md` — 5-layer state management\n- `../spec/schemas/` — published Task-Spec, TaskPlan, TaskHandoff, AuthoringEvidence, and agent-contract schemas\n- `patterns/runnable-bash-evals.md` — eval writing patterns\n- `patterns/validation-card-yaml.md` — YAML contract patterns\n- `patterns/atomic-status-transitions.md` — transition protocol\n- `patterns/anti-patterns-extraction.md` — mining from MCP research\n- `patterns/do-not-touch-detection.md` — repo-scan patterns\n- `runbooks/from-fuzzy-intent.md` — paragraph → Task-Spec\n- `runbooks/decomposing-intent.md` — **v3** intent/PRD/set-of-calls → N linked atomic specs\n- `runbooks/from-meeting-note.md` — Krisp output → Task-Spec\n- `runbooks/from-existing-task.md` — legacy task → v2.1 conversion\n- `runbooks/batch-sprint-compose.md` — bulk intent list → N Task-Spec stubs\n- `runbooks/validating-a-task-spec.md` — linting walkthrough\n- `runbooks/recovering-from-crash.md` — state recovery\n- `runbooks/empirical-experiment-protocol.md` — SDD vs EDD test design\n\n---\n\n## Remember\n\n> **\"Specs that verify themselves keep humans at the edges of the loop — at\n> intent-setting and acceptance review — not in the middle of every iteration.\"**\n\n**Mission:** Be the cornerstone primitive — the open, atomic, vendor-neutral,\nself-verifying unit-of-work format that any conformant agentic system can produce,\nconsume, and trust. Humans enter at intent and at acceptance review; the execution\nloop in between runs without them.\n\n> **On trust:** the HMAC sign-off envelope is **tamper-evident, not tamper-proof**\n> — it proves a spec's evals were not edited after the gate stamped them, with a\n> repo-shared key. It is a drift/accidental-edit guard, not a security boundary.\n> \"Humans at intent + review\" is the honest claim; \"no humans\" is not.\n","numLines":521,"startLine":1,"totalLines":521}}}
