{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf45V7AU2j13fLZPKgXRo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The spec is authored and ready for your review — unsigned, unexecuted, uncommitted.\n\n**`tasks/T-20260914-rate-limit-policy-schema.md`** — one S leaf covering step 1 of the blueprint's steel thread only: a versioned policy schema that rejects invalid limits and windows. Resolution (step 2), denial (step 3), and telemetry (step 4) are explicitly excluded in the anti-patterns, so the atom owns one outcome with a 2-file write surface (`src/policy/schema.py`, `tests/test_policy_schema.py`).\n\n**Four behaviors, five executable evals:**\n\n| Eval | Proves |\n|---|---|\n| `eval_1` | a supported-version, in-domain document normalizes (B-1) |\n| `eval_2` | `0, -1, 1000001, True, False, \"100\", 1.5, None` → `invalid_limit`/`limit` (B-2) |\n| `eval_3` | `0, -60, 86401, True, False, \"60\", 60.5, None` → `invalid_window`/`window_seconds` (B-3) |\n| `eval_4` | missing/empty/unsupported version → `unsupported_schema_version`, never defaulted (B-4) |\n| `eval_5` | the executor's own unit tests exist and pass (B-1..B-4) |\n\n**Verification performed** (no authorization, no acceptance):\n- `taskspec validate` → valid Task-Spec v3, profile standard, status ready\n- `taskspec dod` → `DOD=COMPLETE`, full behavior↔eval traceability both ways\n- `taskspec run --ci` in-repo → all 5 fail for the right reason (`No module named 'policy'`), no bash-level errors\n- In a throwaway directory outside this repo, I ran the evals against a plausible-but-wrong stub (coerces `\"100\"`, defaults the version, admits `bool`) and against a correct implementation. The stub passed `eval_1` and its own weak `eval_5` but was caught by `eval_2`/`eval_3`/`eval_4`; the correct implementation passed all five. The evals discriminate, and the spec is satisfiable. The probe was deleted.\n\n**Assumptions I made, flagged for your review** — all stated in the spec's Context and pinned by the evals, since the blueprint names none of them: Python 3 stdlib only (no YAML/pydantic/jsonschema, so evals run offline in a repo with no package manifest); the document is a plain dict; `limit ∈ 1..1000000`, `window_seconds ∈ 1..86400`, `bool` excluded, no string/float coercion; `SUPPORTED_SCHEMA_VERSIONS == (\"1.0\",)` with no default. If any of those should differ, change the spec before the gate — the numbers live in the evals.\n\nTwo lifecycle notes: `taskspec status` reports the next command as `gate --stamp`, which I deliberately did not run — that stamp is human authorization and is yours. Also, the documented `plan --manifest` → `batch --plan` route isn't usable here: the engine's manifest parser rejects both folded (`>-`) and literal (`|`) block scalars, so multi-line bash evals can't survive a TaskPlan. I used the direct one-task authoring path the skill preserves, and removed the draft manifest rather than leave a manifest the engine rejects."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":666,"cache_read_input_tokens":69560,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":666},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0abf1529-dfad-44b4-923a-1389e8368909","uuid":"3a5614f9-3592-4dfa-ac8d-88c8d193bd22","timestamp":"2026-09-14T21:56:12.716Z","request_id":"req_011Cf45V6fSuNznNFEBPGbsU"}
